== 1. The Great: Self-Seeking

#align(center)[The fourth thing to be shown is the various ways and devices that Satan has to destroy and ensnare all sorts and ranks of men in the world.]

I will begin with the honorable and the great, and show you the devices that Satan has to destroy them. I will cite only those that are most important.

#strong[His first device to destroy the great and honorable of the earth is,]

BY working on them to make it their business to seek themselves: to seek how to make themselves great, to raise#footnote[Self-seeking, like the deluge, overflows the whole world.] themselves, to enrich themselves, to secure themselves, etc., as you may see in Pharaoh, Ahab, Rehoboam, Jeroboam, Absalom, Joab, Haman, etc. But were the Scripture silent, our own experience abundantly proves this way and method of Satan to destroy the great and the honorable, to bury their names in the dust and their souls in hell: by drawing them to mind themselves wholly, and to mind only themselves, and to mind themselves in all things, and to mind themselves always. “All” (says the apostle) “mind#footnote[Phil 2:21.] themselves.” All, comparatively, in respect of the fewness of others who let their private interests fall, and drown all regard for self in the glory of God and the public good, etc.

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is, solemnly to consider#footnote[Self-love is the root of hatred of others. 2 Tim 3:2: first “lovers of themselves,” and then “fierce,” etc.] that self-seeking is a sin that will drive men to a world of sin: to sins not only against the law of God and the rules of the gospel, but against the very laws of nature, which are so much darkened by the fall of man. It drove the Pharisees to oppose Christ, and Judas to betray Christ, and Pilate#footnote[The naturalist observes that the beasts that are most cruel to others are most loving to their own.] to condemn Christ. It drove Gehazi to lying, and Balaam to cursing, and Saul and Absalom to plotting David’s ruin. It drove Pharaoh and Haman to contrive ways to destroy those Jews whom God purposed to save by his mighty arm. It drives men to use wicked balances and the bag of deceitful weights. It drives men to ways of oppression, “to sell#footnote[Amos 2:6.] the righteous for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals,” etc. I know of no sin in the world that this sin of self-seeking will not drive men to, though it be their eternal loss.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is, seriously to consider that self-seeking exceedingly abases#footnote[A self-seeker is a Cato outside, but a Nero within. Domitian would seem to love best those whom he least wished to live, and that is the very spirit of self-seekers.] a man. It strips him of all his royalty and glory. From a lord it makes a man a servant to created things, yes, often to the worst of creatures; indeed, a slave to slaves, as you may see in Judas, Demas, Balaam, and the scribes and Pharisees. Self-seekers bow down to created things, as Gideon’s many thousands bowed down to the waters. Self-seeking will make a man say anything, do anything, and be anything, to please the lusts of others and to gain advantages over others. Self-seeking transforms a man into all shapes and forms. Now#footnote[It was death under the rites of Moses to counterfeit that ceremonial and figurative ointment (Exod 30). What then will it be to counterfeit the spirit of life and holiness?] it makes a man appear as an angel of light, and soon after as an angel of darkness. Now self-seekers are seemingly for God, and soon after they are openly against God. Now you will have them crying “Hosanna in the highest,” and soon after “Crucify him, crucify him!” Now you will have them building with the saints, and soon after you will have them plotting the overthrow of the saints, as those self-seekers did in the time of Ezra and Nehemiah. Self-seekers are the basest of all persons; there is no service so base, so poor, so low, that they will not bow to it. They cannot look either above or beyond their own lusts and the enjoyment#footnote[Rom 1:25.] of created things; these are the first and final objects of their aims.

It is said of Tiberius that while Augustus ruled, his reputation was in no way tainted; and that while Drusus and Germanicus were alive, he feigned virtues that he did not have, to keep a good opinion of himself in the hearts of the people. But after he had got himself out of the reach of contradiction and control, there was no deed in which he was not at fault, no crime to which he was not an accessory. My prayer will be that the spirit of Tiberius may not be found in any of our rulers, lest it prove their ruin, as it did his; and that wherever it is, it may be detected, loathed, and cast out, so that neither the state nor souls may be ruined by it, etc.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is, solemnly to dwell on those dreadful curses and woes that are pronounced from heaven against self-seekers. “Woe to you#super[+] who add#footnote[Isa 5:8.] house to house and join field to field until no place is left and you live alone in the land.” So Habakkuk:#footnote[Hab 2:6, 9, 10, 11, 12. Crassus, a very rich Roman and a great self-seeker, waged war against the Parthians out of greedy desire for gold, and by them both he and 30,000 Romans were slain. And because the barbarians guessed that he had made this assault on them for their gold, they melted gold and poured it into his dead body, saying (#emph[satura te auro]), “Satisfy yourself with gold.” Isa 10:1, 2.] “Woe to him who amasses what is not his and makes himself rich with many loans! … Woe to him who builds his house by unjust gain, to place his nest on high and escape the hand of disaster! You have plotted shame for your house by cutting off many peoples and forfeiting your life. For the stones will cry out from the wall, and the beam from the woodwork will answer it. Woe to him who builds a city with bloodshed and establishes a town by iniquity!” The materials of the house built up by oppression will come as joint witnesses. The stones of the wall will cry, “Lord, we were built up by blood and violence”; and the beam will answer, “True, Lord, even so it is.” The stones will cry, “Vengeance, Lord, on these self-seekers”; and the beam will answer, “Woe to him, because he built his house with blood.” So Isaiah: “Woe to those who enact unjust statutes and issue oppressive decrees, to deprive the poor of fair treatment and withhold justice from the oppressed of My people, to make widows their prey and orphans their plunder.” So Amos: “Woe#footnote[Amos 6:1, 3–6.] to those at ease in Zion and those secure on Mount Samaria, the distinguished ones of the foremost nation, to whom the house of Israel comes. You#super[+] dismiss the day of calamity and bring near a reign of violence. You#super[+] lie on beds inlaid with ivory, and lounge upon your couches. You#super[+] dine on lambs from the flock and calves from the stall. … You#super[+] drink wine by the bowlful and anoint yourselves with the finest oils, but you fail to grieve over the ruin of Joseph.” So Micah: “Woe to those who devise iniquity#footnote[Mic 2:1, 2. The word of Tacitus, the Roman emperor, was (#emph[sibi bonus aliis malus]): he who is too much for himself fails to be good to others.] and plot evil on their beds! At morning’s light they accomplish it because the power is in their hands. They covet fields and seize them; they take away houses. They deprive a man of his home, a fellow man of his inheritance.”

By these Scriptures you see that self-seekers labor like a woman in childbirth, but their birth proves their death; their pleasure, their pain; their comfort, their torment; their glory, their shame; their exaltation, their desolation. Loss, disgrace, trouble, and shame, vexation and confusion will be the certain portion of self-seekers. When the Tartars had taken the duke of Muscovy in battle, they made a cup of his skull, with this inscription: #emph[All covet, all lose.]

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is, solemnly to consider that self-seekers are self-losers and#footnote[Adam seeks himself, and loses himself, Paradise, and that blessed image that God had stamped on him. Lot seeks himself (Gen 13:10, 11) and loses himself and his goods, etc. Peter seeks to save himself, and miserably loses himself. Hezekiah, in the business of the ambassadors, seeks himself, and loses himself, and would have lost his life too, had not God saved him by a miracle.] self-destroyers. Absalom and Judas seek themselves and hang themselves. Saul seeks himself and kills himself. Ahab seeks himself and loses himself, his crown, and his kingdom. Pharaoh seeks himself and overthrows himself and his mighty army in the Red Sea. Cain sought himself and slew two at once, his brother and his own soul. Gehazi sought a change of clothing, but God changed his clothing into a leprous skin. Haman sought himself and lost himself. The princes and the administrators sought themselves (in the ruin of Daniel), but ruined themselves, their wives, and their children. What self-seekers think should be a staff to support them becomes (by the hand of justice) an iron rod to break them; what they would have as springs to refresh them becomes a gulf to consume them utterly. The crosses of self-seekers will always exceed their mercies; their pain, their pleasure; their torments, their comforts. Every self-seeker is a self-tormentor, a self-destroyer; he carries a hell, an executioner, in his own bosom, etc.

The fifth remedy against this device of Satan is, to dwell much on the famous examples of those worthy saints who have denied themselves and preferred the public good before their own private advantage. As Moses: the #smallcaps[Lord] said to Moses, “Leave Me alone, so that I may destroy them and blot out their name from under heaven. Then I will make you into a nation mightier and greater than they are.” Oh, but this offer would#footnote[It is good to be of the opinion and mind of him who was more willing to beautify Italy than his own house.] not win Moses. Being a man of a noble public spirit, he is hot in his desires and prayers that the people might be spared and pardoned. He says, “Pardon, I pray, the iniquity of this people, in keeping with the greatness of Your loving devotion, just as You have forgiven them ever since they left Egypt.” And the #smallcaps[Lord] said, “I have pardoned them as you requested.” Ah, should God make such an offer to many who style themselves Moses, and are called Moses by many, I am afraid they would prefer their own advantage above the#footnote[The ancients used to place the statues of their princes by their fountains, implying that they were (or at least should be) fountains of the public good.] public good. They would not care what became of the people, so long as they and theirs might be made great and glorious in the world. They would not care, so long as they might have a Babel built for them, though it was on the ashes and ruin of the people. There are no baser spirits than these in hell, no, not in hell; and I am sure there are no such spirits in heaven. Such men’s hearts and principles must be changed, or they will be ruined forever. Nehemiah was a#footnote[A certain great emperor, coming into Egypt, to show the zeal he had for the public good, said to the Egyptians, “Draw from me as from your river Nile.”] choice soul, a man of a noble public spirit, a man who spent his time, his strength, and his estate for the good and ease of his people. “Furthermore” (says he), “from the day King Artaxerxes appointed me to be their governor in the land of Judah, from his twentieth year until his thirty-second year (twelve years total), neither I nor my brothers ate the food allotted to the governor. … Also I devoted myself to the construction of the wall, and all my servants were gathered there for the work; we did not acquire any land. There were 150 Jews and officials at my table, besides the guests from the surrounding nations. Each day one ox, six choice sheep, and some fowl were prepared for me, and once every ten days an abundance of all kinds of wine was provided. But I did not demand the food allotted to the governor, because the burden on the people was so heavy. Remember me favorably, O my God, for all that I have done for this people.” So Daniel was a man of a noble public spirit: “Then the administrators and satraps#footnote[The Counselor says a statesman should be divided thus into three parts: his will to God, his love to his master, his heart to his country, his secret to his friend, his time to business.] sought a charge against Daniel concerning the kingdom, but they could find no charge or corruption, because he was trustworthy, and no negligence or corruption was found in him. Finally these men said, ‘We will never find any charge against this Daniel, unless we find something against him concerning the law of his God.’”

Christ had a public spirit; he laid himself out, and laid himself down, for a public good. Oh, never stop looking at and meditating on these precious and sweet examples, till your#super[+] souls are enlivened and raised up to act for the public good more than for your own private advantage. Many#footnote[Solomon’s judgment seat was propped up with lions, to show what spirit and mettle a magistrate should be made of.] heathens have been excellent at this. Macrobius writes of Augustus Caesar (in whose time Christ was born) that he bore such an entire and fatherly affection to the commonwealth that he called it (#emph[filiam suam]) his own daughter. And therefore he refused to be called (#emph[Dominus]) the lord or master of his country, and would only be called (#emph[pater patriae]) father of his country, because he governed it not by fear (#emph[per timorem sed per amorem]) but by love; and the senate and the people of Rome together hailed him by the name (#emph[pater patriae]) Father of his Country. The people greatly lamented his death, using this saying: “Would he had#footnote[Utinam aut non nasceretur, aut non moreretur.] never been born, or never died.” So Marcus Regulus, to save his country from ruin, exposed himself to the greatest sufferings that the malice and rage of his enemies could inflict. So Titus and Aristides, and many others, have been famous for preferring the public good above their own advantage. My prayer is, and will be, that all our rulers may be so filled with this spirit by God that they may be willing to be anything, to be nothing, to deny themselves, and to trample their sinful selves underfoot, for the honor of God and a public good; so that neither saints nor heathens may be witnesses against them in that day in which the hearts and practices of all the rulers in the world will be open and bare before him who will judge the world in righteousness and justice, etc.

The sixth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is, seriously to consider that self is a great hindrance to divine things; that is why the prophets and apostles were usually carried out of themselves when they had their clearest, choicest, highest, and most glorious visions. Self-seeking blinds the soul, so that it cannot see a beauty in Christ, nor an excellence in holiness. It disorders the palate, so that a man cannot taste sweetness in the word of God, nor in the ways of God, nor in the company of the people of God. It shuts the hand against all the soul-enriching offers of Christ; it hardens the heart against all the knocks and entreaties of Christ; it makes the soul like an empty vine and a barren wilderness. “Israel is an empty#footnote[Hos 10:1.] vine, yielding fruit for himself.” There is nothing that shows a man to be more empty and void of God, Christ, and grace than self-seeking. The Pharisees#footnote[Self-seekers, with Esau, prefer a bowl of stew above their birthright, and with the men of Shechem value the bramble above the vine, the olive, and the fig tree; yes, empty things above a full Christ, and base things above a glorious Christ.] were great self-seekers, and great undervaluers of Christ, his word, and his Spirit. There is no greater hindrance to all the duties of piety than self-seeking. Oh, this is what keeps many a soul from seeking after God and the precious things of eternity. They cannot wait on God, nor act for God, nor abide in those ways in which they might meet with God, because of self. Self-seeking is what drives many a man to neglect and slight the things of his peace. Self-seekers will neither go into heaven themselves, nor allow others to enter who are ready to take the kingdom by force, as you may see in the scribes and Pharisees. Oh, but a gracious spirit is moved quite otherwise, as you may see in that sweet Scripture, Song 7:13: “At our gates are all kinds of choice fruits, new#footnote[Song 7:13.] as well as old, that I have laid up for you, my beloved.” All that the church has and is, is#footnote[The saints’ motto is, #emph[Propter te, Domine, propter te] \[For you, Lord, for you\].] only for him. Let others bear fruit to themselves and lay up for themselves; gracious spirits will hide for Christ and lay up for Christ. All the divine endeavors and productions of saints fall into God’s bosom and empty themselves into his lap. As Christ lays up his merits for them, his graces for them, his comforts for them, his crown for them, so they lay up all their fruits, and all their loves, all their graces, and#footnote[The saints’ motto is, #emph[non nobis Domine] \[not to us, O Lord\].] all their experience, and all their services, only for him who is the soul of their comforts, and the crown and top of all their royalty and glory, etc.
